# Whether the arrow pointer reads on every background (vtium/vtium#102),
# and the shapes a program sets with OSC 22 (vtium/vtium#59).
# vtium draws the pointer as an arrow. #102 would draw it by inverting the
# pixels under it instead, so it reads on any background. The first steps
# show backgrounds where an arrow might be hard to see; their answers
# decide whether #102 is needed. The later steps have a program set each
# of vtium's pointer shapes, push and pop them, and ask which shape shows.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=pointer

title: the pointer on every background, and its shapes

step: White, black and mid gray
run: clear; for c in 107 40 100; do for i in $(seq 1 8); do printf "\033[${c}m%*s\033[0m\n" "$(tput cols)" ""; done; done; sleep 30
expect: three bands fill the screen: white, black and mid gray. Move the mouse
expect: across all three. The arrow is *easy to see on each band*, and its tip
expect: is clear enough to aim at a single character.

step: Strong colors
run: clear; for c in 41 42 43 44 45 46; do for i in $(seq 1 4); do printf "\033[${c}m%*s\033[0m\n" "$(tput cols)" ""; done; done; sleep 30
expect: six bands: red, green, yellow, blue, magenta and cyan. Move the mouse
expect: across every band. The arrow is *easy to see on each one*.

step: Busy text
run: clear; for i in $(seq 1 24); do printf '\033[3%dm%s\033[0m\n' $((i % 8)) "$(head -c 400 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0 | cut -c1-$(tput cols))"; done; sleep 30
expect: the screen is full of dense colored characters. Move the mouse over
expect: the text. You can *follow the arrow* without losing it.

step: Each shape a program sets
run: clear; for s in default text pointer crosshair wait progress not-allowed move ew-resize ns-resize; do clear; printf '\033]22;%s\033\\' "$s"; printf '\033[7m%*s\033[0m\n' "$(tput cols)" ""; echo "The pointer shows the shape: $s"; sleep 6; done; printf '\033]22;\033\\'; clear
do: move the mouse over the screen while the shapes change, every six seconds.
expect: the pointer takes ten shapes in turn, each named on the screen: the
expect: arrow, an *I-beam*, a *hand* with a raised finger, a thin cross, an
expect: *hourglass*, the arrow with a small hourglass, a *circle with a slash*,
expect: four arrows, a double arrow *left and right* and a double arrow
expect: *up and down*. Each one is white with a dark outline, and reads on the black
expect: screen and on the white bar.

step: A shape's point is where the pointer is
run: clear; printf '\033]22;crosshair\033\\'; for i in $(seq 1 6); do printf '%*s+\n\n' $((i * 8)) ""; done; sleep 30; printf '\033]22;\033\\'; clear
do: put the centre of the crosshair on each + in turn and double-click.
expect: the crosshair's centre is the point the pointer names: the + under the
expect: centre is *the character that shows selected* after each double-click.

step: Push, pop and the alternate screen
run: clear; printf '\033]22;>text\033\\\033]22;>pointer\033\\'; echo "the hand, for ten seconds"; sleep 10; printf '\033]22;<\033\\'; echo "after a pop: the I-beam, for ten seconds"; sleep 10; printf '\033[?1049h\033[H'; echo "the alternate screen: the arrow, for ten seconds"; sleep 10; printf '\033[?1049l'; echo "back on the main screen: the I-beam again"; sleep 10; printf '\033c'; echo "after a reset: the arrow"; sleep 8
do: keep the mouse moving over the screen while the lines appear.
expect: the pointer is *the hand*, then *the I-beam* after the pop. On the
expect: alternate screen it is *the arrow*, and back on the main screen it is
expect: *the I-beam* again. After the reset it is *the arrow*.

step: An unknown name gives the arrow
run: clear; printf '\033]22;pointer\033\\'; echo "the hand"; sleep 6; printf '\033]22;no-such-shape\033\\'; echo "an unknown name: the arrow"; sleep 8; printf '\033]22;\033\\'
do: move the mouse while the lines appear.
expect: the pointer is the hand, then *the arrow* once a program names a shape
expect: vtium does not have.

step: A query answers from vtium's own shapes
run: clear; printf '\033]22;hand2\033\\'; stty -echo -icanon; printf '\033]22;?__current__,text,no-such-shape\033\\'; IFS= read -r -t 2 -d '\\' reply; stty sane; printf 'the reply: %q\n' "$reply"; printf '\033]22;\033\\'; sleep 10
expect: the screen prints the reply as *$'\E]22;pointer,1,0\E'*: the shape set
expect: by its X cursor name hand2 is named pointer, text is a shape vtium
expect: has, and no-such-shape is not. The reply *never repeats* hand2 or
expect: no-such-shape.
